      "id": 1,
      "text": "Steely Hollow takes its name from Steeley Springs, a natural water source that once served as a vital stop for early pioneers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/steely_hollow_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early settlement in Steely Hollow is closely linked to the history of the Cherokee Nation following the Trail of Tears in the late 1830s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/steely_hollow_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Cherokee Nation members first settled the fertile lands of Steely Hollow by focusing on subsistence farming and community building during the 1840s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/steely_hollow_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "European-American settlement in Steely Hollow began around the turn of the 20th century following land allotments under the Dawes Act.",
